---
import AdSlot from "../components/AdSlot.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Editorial Standards — PULSE"
  description="How PULSE sources, edits, corrects and separates editorial coverage from advertising."
>
  <div class="container-page-wide flex justify-center pt-4 pb-2">
    <AdSlot slot="leaderboard-top" format="leaderboard" label="Advertisement" />
  </div>

  <section class="container-page max-w-[720px] pt-10 pb-14">
    <span class="eyebrow eyebrow-accent">About PULSE</span>
    <h1 class="section-title mt-2 text-[clamp(28px,4.5vw,40px)]">Editorial standards</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink-3">
      These are the rules our newsroom operates under. They apply to every story, every section, every
      byline — no exceptions for sponsors, subjects or subscribers.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-col gap-9">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl">Sourcing</h2>
        <p class="mt-2.5 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-3">
          We attribute information to a named, on-record source wherever possible. Anonymous sources are
          used only when a source faces real risk for speaking on the record, and only when an editor has
          independently verified their access to the information. We do not publish single-source claims
          about wrongdoing without corroboration.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl">Editorial independence</h2>
        <p class="mt-2.5 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-3">
          Advertisers, sponsors and PULSE+ subscribers have no input into what we cover or how we cover it.
          Sponsored content is labeled "Sponsored" in the layout itself — not in a footnote — and is never
          written or edited by the newsroom.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl">Corrections</h2>
        <p class="mt-2.5 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-3">
          When we get something wrong, we say so. Factual errors are corrected as soon as they're
          confirmed, with a note on the affected article describing what changed. See our
          <a href="/corrections" class="font-bold text-primary hover:underline">corrections policy</a> for
          how to flag an error.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl">Use of AI</h2>
        <p class="mt-2.5 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-3">
          Reporting, writing and editing at PULSE is done by our journalists. We may use AI tools for
          research assistance, transcription or headline experimentation, but a human editor is
          accountable for every published word.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl">Bylines and accountability</h2>
        <p class="mt-2.5 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-3">
          Every story carries the name of the reporter(s) who wrote it. If you have a concern about a
          specific piece, the fastest path is the reporter's author page, which lists their full
          archive — or you can reach the newsroom directly through
          <a href="/contact" class="font-bold text-primary hover:underline">Contact &amp; tips</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
